course: chemistry
lesson: 20
title: Gases all around: breath and balloons
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Breathe on a cold mirror and a mist appears. Your breath is full of invisible gases: water vapour, which turns to tiny droplets on the cold glass, and carbon dioxide, which your body makes as it uses food.
explain: In class, a teacher can have you breathe out gently through a straw into clear lime water, and it goes milky: carbon dioxide reacts with it and makes tiny bits of chalk. Ordinary air has so little carbon dioxide that pumping it in hardly clouds the water.
explain: Gases are made of tiny particles zooming about. Heat them and they move faster and spread out, which is why a hot-air balloon rises; cool them and they slow down, which is why a car tyre looks softer on a frosty morning.
explain: Helium atoms are so tiny they slip through the rubber skin of a party balloon, so it shrinks in a few days. Some gases you smell, like rotten eggs, but some, like carbon monoxide from engines, have no smell at all and can kill, which is why homes have carbon monoxide alarms.
fact: Breath contains water vapour, which condenses on a cold mirror; the body makes water when it uses food, and you breathe out about a glass of water a day.
fact: Breath has much more carbon dioxide than ordinary air; carbon dioxide turns lime water milky by making tiny chalk particles, a test scientists use.
fact: Helium atoms are so small they slip through rubber, so helium balloons shrink after a few days; foil balloons last longer because their skin has smaller gaps.
fact: Released balloons come down far away, do not break down quickly and can harm animals that eat them; many places now ban balloon releases.
fact: A hot-air balloon rises because heated air spreads out, so the same space holds less air and is lighter than the cold air outside; the burner keeps the air hot.
fact: The first hot-air balloon passengers, in 1783, were a sheep, a duck and a cockerel.
fact: Rotten eggs smell because bacteria break down proteins with sulphur in them and give off hydrogen sulphide gas; noses can sense tiny amounts of it, and some hot springs smell of it too.
fact: The white cloud from a car exhaust on a cold morning is mostly water vapour; burning petrol also makes carbon dioxide and some harmful gases.
fact: Carbon monoxide has no smell and can kill, so a car must never run in a closed garage and carbon monoxide alarms are useful at home.
fact: A fish-tank pump bubbles air into the water, and some oxygen from the bubbles dissolves for the fish.
fact: Water plants use light to make sugar and give off oxygen, so pondweed makes bubbles in sunlight and stops in the dark.
fact: Cold gas particles move slower and hit the walls less hard, so tyres have lower pressure in cold weather and drivers check them more often in winter.
exercise: Why does a mirror mist up when you breathe on it? | Water vapour in breath condenses on the cold glass | Breath carries tiny bits of dust that stick to the glass | The mirror sweats when it is warmed | Carbon dioxide freezes on the mirror
exercise: Where does the water in your breath come from? | Your body makes it when it uses food | It comes from rain and puddles you breathed in | It is spit flying out of your mouth | It is made by the mirror itself
exercise: What happens when you breathe out into lime water? | It goes milky | It turns bright pink | It starts to boil | It freezes solid
exercise: Why does pumping ordinary air into lime water hardly cloud it? | Ordinary air has much less carbon dioxide | Ordinary air has no water in it at all to mix | The pump is too slow to work | Lime water only reacts with warm air
exercise: Why does a helium balloon shrink after a few days? | Tiny helium atoms slip through the rubber | The helium slowly turns into drops of water | The balloon gets cold and wrinkles | The air outside squeezes it flat
exercise: Why do foil balloons float longer than rubber ones? | Their skin has smaller gaps | They hold heavier gas | They are filled at the factory with oxygen | Foil makes its own helium
exercise: What happens to a balloon after it floats out of sight? | It comes down somewhere and can harm animals | It floats up into space for ever and ever | It melts away in the clouds | It turns into rain
exercise: Why does a hot-air balloon rise? | Hot air inside is lighter than cold air outside | The burner pushes it straight up like a rocket | Hot air is full of helium | The basket is lighter than air
exercise: Why does the pilot keep firing the burner? | To keep the air inside hot | To light the way for the basket at night | To scare birds away | To make the balloon spin
exercise: Who were the first hot-air balloon passengers in 1783? | A sheep, a duck and a cockerel | Two French brothers and their dog | A king and a queen | A cat, a mouse and a horse
exercise: What gas gives rotten eggs their smell? | Hydrogen sulphide | Carbon dioxide gas | Helium | Oxygen
exercise: What is the white cloud from a car exhaust on a cold morning mostly? | Water vapour | Black smoke | Carbon monoxide | Helium
exercise: Why are carbon monoxide alarms useful at home? | The gas has no smell but can kill | The gas smells far too bad to bear | The gas makes everyone sneeze | The gas turns walls black
exercise: Why do fish tanks have a bubbling pump? | Some oxygen from the bubbles dissolves for the fish | The fish like to play and chase each other in the bubbles | It keeps the water warm | It makes the water clear by magic
exercise: What happens to the bubbles from pondweed if you move a lamp closer? | More bubbles, as the plant gets more light | The bubbles stop completely | The bubbles turn green | Nothing at all changes
exercise: Why do car tyres seem flatter on frosty mornings? | Cold gas particles move slower and push less | The rubber shrinks and goes hard in the frosty cold | Frost leaks into the tyre | Cold air escapes through the valve
test: "i breathed on the window and drew a smiley in it. where'd the wet come from??" | Water vapour in your breath | Rain on the other side | The glass made it | Spit from your mouth only
test: Ellie says helium balloons go down because the helium "gets tired". What's true? | Tiny helium atoms escape through the rubber | Helium loses all its lift after a day | The balloon fills up with water | Air pushes the helium out through the knot
test: The party planner wants to release 100 balloons into the sky. What's the problem? | They come down and can harm animals | They will block aeroplanes | They will burst the clouds | Nothing, balloons vanish in the sky
test: If the burner of a hot-air balloon stopped working high in the sky, what would happen? | The air inside would cool and the balloon would sink | The balloon would shoot up faster | The balloon would stay still for ever | The balloon would burst at once
test: "dad's gonna fix the car in the garage with the engine running and the door shut". What's the danger? | Carbon monoxide builds up, with no smell | The garage will get far too cold to work in | The car will run out of petrol | Nothing, exhaust is just steam
test: In class, Sam pumps air into lime water and nothing much happens; then he breathes into it and it goes milky. Why the difference? | Breath has much more carbon dioxide | Breath is warmer, and lime water likes heat | Sam's breath had sweets in it | The pump was broken
test: "the hot spring stinks of rotten eggs, did someone drop eggs in it?" | No, it gives off the same gas, hydrogen sulphide | Yes, people throw eggs in for luck every single day | No, it is the smell of hot water | Yes, birds lay eggs in the spring
test: In a cartoon, a balloon is filled with hot air and then left in a freezer, where it floats up. What would really happen? | The air would cool and the balloon would shrink and sink | It would float higher in the cold | It would turn into ice and fly | It would burst from the cold
test: Pondweed in a jar on the windowsill makes bubbles all day. What happens at night? | The bubbles stop | The bubbles get faster | The bubbles turn red | The jar fills with water vapour
test: "tyre was fine yesterday, looks flat this morning, it's minus 5". What's the likely reason? | Cold air in it pushes less on the walls | A hedgehog bit a small hole in it during the night | Frost has leaked into it | Cold makes the tyre grow bigger
test: Which balloon will still be floating after a week? | A foil balloon | A rubber helium balloon | A balloon blown up with breath | A water balloon
exam: Identify the substance whose presence in exhaled breath causes lime water to become milky. | Carbon dioxide | Oxygen | Nitrogen | Helium
exam: It is given that the air within a balloon is heated. Select the statement most accurately characterising the consequence. | The air expands, becomes lighter than the surrounding air, and the balloon rises | The air contracts and the balloon descends | The air is converted into helium | The air becomes heavier and more compressed, and the balloon remains wholly stationary
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises carbon monoxide? | It is odourless and can prove fatal | It possesses a strong odour of rotten eggs | It is entirely harmless in enclosed spaces | It is visible as a white cloud
exam: Ascertain the reason for which a helium balloon of rubber diminishes in size over several days. | Helium atoms pass through minute gaps in the rubber | Helium is converted into water vapour | The rubber absorbs the helium chemically | Atmospheric pressure crushes the balloon
